<h2>A three-page judgment, by design</h2>
<p>The petition under Section 11 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 sought the appointment of a sole arbitrator. By the time the matter was heard, counsel for both parties agreed that the disputes could be referred. The court recorded two findings and no more: that the scope of enquiry under Section 11 is confined to examining the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement, and that there was no dispute about the existence of a valid arbitration clause between the parties. On that footing, the appointment followed.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The operative directions are a useful template of current Delhi practice: the sole arbitrator to enter upon the reference within three weeks; disclosures under Section 12(2) within three weeks of entering the reference; the arbitration to be conducted under the rules and aegis of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, with fees per the DIAC schedule; all pleas, including arbitrability and jurisdiction, open before the tribunal; and the tribunal to decide uninfluenced by any observation in the appointment order.</p>
</div>

<h2>The shrinking of Section 11 — and why it matters</h2>
<p>A generation ago, appointment petitions were trials in miniature: courts examined live claims, dead claims, accord and satisfaction, limitation. The 2015 insertion of Section 11(6A) confined the enquiry to the existence of the agreement, and the Supreme Court's subsequent jurisprudence — through the referral-stage decisions and the seven-judge bench on arbitration clauses in unstamped agreements — settled that the referral court looks only for a prima facie arbitration agreement, leaving everything substantive to the tribunal under Section 16.</p>
<p>The practical consequence appears in exactly this kind of order. Where the clause's existence is undisputed, the petition can and should be disposed of in a single hearing. Respondents gain nothing by contesting the reference on grounds the tribunal must decide anyway; petitioners gain nothing by over-pleading. The real contest simply relocates to the arbitration.</p>

<h2>What each side should take from the order</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Before filing.</strong> Serve a proper notice invoking arbitration under Section 21 and allow thirty days; a premature petition invites dismissal on a ground entirely unrelated to the merits.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>At the hearing.</strong> If the clause is genuine, consider consenting to the reference while expressly reserving jurisdictional objections — the course taken here preserves every defence and saves a contested hearing.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>After appointment.</strong> Scrutinise the Section 12(2) disclosure; independence objections have a statutory window and a statutory route under Sections 12 and 13.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>On fees.</strong> Institutional schedules such as the DIAC's displace ad hoc fee negotiation; parties should budget from the schedule against the quantum of claims and counterclaims.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Keep Section 11 petitions lean: the agreement, the invocation notice, the failure of the agreed mechanism, and the prayer. Substantive grievances belong in the statement of claim.</p>
<p>Reserving pleas before the arbitrator is not a formality — raise jurisdictional objections under Section 16 at the earliest, ordinarily not later than the statement of defence.</p>
<p>Diary the tribunal's timelines from the date it enters the reference; Section 29A's clock governs domestic awards and extensions need judicial or, where applicable, institutional sanction.</p>
</div>
